\title{PanelSelect: panel sample-selection models}
\description{
PanelSelect provides a unified and flexible framework for sample selection
models with continuous, binary, and count outcomes in panel and cross-sectional
data. In particular, it supports a broad family of panel selection models,
which extend the classic Heckman framework to account for persistent
unobserved heterogeneity. The package supports selection at two levels:
the individual level, through correlated random effects, and the observation
level, through correlated occasion-specific shocks. This allows researchers
to distinguish non-random selection driven by persistent individual
characteristics from selection driven by temporary shocks. Alongside model
estimation, PanelSelect supports prediction of population-level outcomes.
}
\section{Main functions}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=panel_select]{panel_select()}} fits a model from an outcome formula, a selection formula
(or \code{NULL} for a standalone model), the data, the panel ID column (or
\code{NULL} for cross-sectional data) and the outcome family; its arguments
also set restrictions, starting values, missing-data handling and the
reported covariance. \code{\link[=panel_control]{panel_control()}} sets the integration rules and the
optimizer.
\item Standard methods (\link{panelselect-methods}) summarize, compare and update fits;
\code{\link[=predict.panelselect]{predict.panelselect()}}, \code{\link[=residuals.panelselect]{residuals.panelselect()}} and \code{\link[=random_effects]{random_effects()}}
give predictions and posterior summaries.
\item \code{\link[=check_quadrature]{check_quadrature()}} compares likelihoods and scores at retained estimates
under higher orders or an independent GL rule; agreement is evidence of
stability, not an integration certificate.
\item \code{\link[=check_stationarity]{check_stationarity()}} reads stored scores and information, reporting
\code{Q_OPG} and, with valid positive-definite stored observed information and
its inverse, \code{Q_Hessian}. The default warning is strictly \code{Q_OPG > 0.1}.
It does not compute a Hessian or alter backend convergence.
\item \code{\link[=add_information]{add_information()}} explicitly returns a fit with observed information
stored for reuse. Requested covariance can remain unavailable; optimizer
convergence, stationarity, integration and inference require distinct checks.
}

Use \code{panel_select()} in place of the PanelSelect 1.x and PanelCount wrappers;
\code{vignette("migration", package = "PanelSelect")} maps their calls and
results to \code{panel_select()}.
}

\section{Quick start}{


\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{fit = panel_select(y ~ x, observed ~ .outcome + z, data, id = "person",
                   family = "linear")
summary(fit)
check_stationarity(fit)[c("Q_OPG", "Q_Hessian")]
predict(fit, newdata, type = "selected_mean")
panel_select(count ~ x, NULL, data, "person", "poisson")  # no selection
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Start with \code{vignette("vignette", package = "PanelSelect")}; users of earlier
versions should read \code{vignette("migration", package = "PanelSelect")}.
GL supports population predictions; history-conditioned predictions and
random-effect summaries require a GH/AGH fit, with no prediction-time
integration override. History-conditioned prediction requires explicit
history; \code{\link[=random_effects]{random_effects()}} defaults to the retained fitting rows.
